/* Nelson Textbook data chunk 06/07; generated only from absi/Nelson.zip. */
window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-007","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"The following drugs can be given with caution to breast-feeding mother","o":["psychotropic drugs","amphetamines","bromocriptine","chloramphenicol","methimazole"],"k":0,"ex":"Most maternal drugs pass into breast milk in small amounts, and the decision depends on the infant's age, the drug's dose and half-life, and the infant's ability to clear it. Premature and newborn infants are the most vulnerable.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. psychotropic drugs — the key; used with caution and infant monitoring.\n\n- B. amphetamines — listed by Nelson as contraindicated (infant stimulation, poor feeding).\n- C. bromocriptine — listed as contraindicated; inhibits lactation.\n- D. chloramphenicol — listed as contraindicated; marrow suppression and gray baby syndrome risk.\n- E. methimazole — Nelson lists it as contraindicated, but current references generally accept low doses with infant thyroid monitoring, which makes it a competing defensible answer.\n\nSource explanation:\n7.(A). Amphetamines, bromocriptine, chloramphenicol, and methimazole are\ncontraindicated in breast-feeding mother.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The text is intact. The review issue is answer-key accuracy: option E conflicts with modern lactation guidance, so the key cannot be treated as certain. Answer Verification Required. Decide whether to keep it with a modified key or exclude it.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q7, PDF p.164; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":7,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-011","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other","q":"with the following conditions EXCEPT","o":["short cord","small fetal size","polyhydramnios","monoamniotic twinning","fetal demise"],"k":0,"ex":"The source explanation for this item says short cords and noncoiled cords occur with chromosome abnormalities and omphalocele. The cord at term is about 55 cm.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. short cord — the keyed option; the source explanation is about short cords.\n\n- B. small fetal size — label lost in OCR, read by sequence; relationship cannot be judged without the stem.\n- C. polyhydramnios — relationship cannot be judged without the stem.\n- D. monoamniotic twinning — relationship cannot be judged without the stem.\n- E. fetal demise — noncoiled cords are linked with intrauterine demise per the source; relationship to the stem cannot be judged.\n\nSource explanation:\n11.(A). Short cords and noncoiled cords occur with chromosome abnormalities and\nomphalocele.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; only 'with the following conditions EXCEPT' survives. The stem was not reconstructed. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q11, PDF pp.164-165; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":11,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-012","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other","q":"Cthe incidence of monozygotic twins is unaffected by racial or familial factors","o":[],"k":null,"ex":"The only readable option is a statement that the incidence of monozygotic twins is unaffected by racial or familial factors, which is true in general: monozygotic twinning occurs at a roughly constant rate (about 3-4 per 1,000 births) worldwide, whereas dizygotic twinning varies with race, maternal age, parity, family history and fertility treatment. The source explanation says spontaneous multifetal gestation has been stable over the years, but the overall rate is rising because of ovarian stimulants (clomiphene,…","more":"Options analysis:\n- Only option C survives: 'the incidence of monozygotic twins is unaffected by racial or familial factors' — scientifically consistent with the general facts above, but whether it is the key or the exception is unknown.\n\n- Options A, B and the stem are missing; option D (the key) is missing.\n- No analysis of unreadable options is given.\n\nSource explanation:\n12.(D). Although the incidence of spontaneous multifetal gestation has been stable\nover the years, the overall incidence of multifetal gestation is increasing as a result of\ntreatment of infertility with ovarian stimulants (clomiphene, gonadotropins) and in\nvitro fertilization. Twins account for about 2.5% of births but about 20% of very low\nbirthweight (VLBW) infants.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem and options A, B and D are missing; the question is unusable until the PDF page is checked.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q12, PDF p.165; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":12,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-013","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"One of the following is a common neonatal respiratory problem associated with premature infants","o":["bronchopulmonary dysplasia","apnea","congenital pneumonia","pneumothorax","pneumomediastinum"],"k":1,"ex":"Prematurity predisposes the newborn to respiratory distress syndrome (surfactant deficiency) and to **apnea of prematurity**, caused by immature respiratory control. Nelson's explanation names RDS and apnea as the common respiratory problems of premature infants, and the key is B (apnea).","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. bronchopulmonary dysplasia — strongly associated with prematurity (chronic, after ventilation); this is the competing defensible answer.\n\n- B. apnea — the key; very common in premature infants (immature respiratory centers).\n- C. congenital pneumonia — not specific to prematurity.\n- D. pneumothorax — air-leak complication, mostly with RDS and ventilation.\n- E. pneumomediastinum — air-leak complication.\n\nSource explanation:\n13.(B). Respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease) and apnea are\ncommon neonatal respiratory problem associated with premature infants.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Text is intact. Multiple defensible answers: option A is also a common respiratory problem of premature infants, and the stem does not ask for the 'most common' or 'earliest'. Key preserved; Answer Verification Required.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q13, PDF p.165; answer on PDF p.179","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":13,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-017","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["pneumothorax","necrotizing enterocolitis","bacterial sepsis","myocarditis","hypoglycemia"],"k":null,"ex":"The source explanation says sudden onset of hypotension in a very low birthweight (VLBW) infant suggests **pneumothorax, intraventricular hemorrhage, or subcapsular hepatic hematoma**. A tension pneumothorax reduces venous return and cardiac output; IVH and liver hematoma cause blood loss and shock.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. pneumothorax — the key and one of the three causes named in the source explanation.\n\n- B. necrotizing enterocolitis — label lost in OCR, read by sequence; can cause shock but is not in the source's list for sudden hypotension.\n- C. bacterial sepsis — causes hypotension, usually with other signs.\n- D. myocarditis — a rare cause.\n- E. hypoglycemia — causes neuroglycopenic signs more than hypotension.\n\nSource explanation:\n17.(A). Sudden onset of hypotension in a very low birthweight (VLBW) infant suggests\npneumothorax, intraventricular hemorrhage, or subcapsular hepatic hematoma.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing. The wording 'sudden onset of hypotension' comes only from the source explanation and is not confirmed as the stem. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q17, PDF pp.165-166; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":17,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-018","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Seizures beginning in the delivery room or shortly thereafter may be due to","o":["hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy","unintentional injection of maternal local anesthetic into the fetus","intracranial hemorrhage","cerebral anomaly","hypoglycemia"],"k":1,"ex":"Nelson's explanation states that seizures starting in the delivery room or soon after may be due to unintentional injection of maternal local anesthetic into the fetus during regional block, and that convulsions may also result from hyponatremia and water intoxication after a large amount of hypotonic fluid is given to the mother. Fetal anesthetic toxicity presents with bradycardia, hypotonia, dilated fixed pupils and seizures soon after birth.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy — the commonest cause of neonatal seizures; typically within the first 12-24 hours.\n\n- B. unintentional injection of maternal local anesthetic — the keyed option; matches the source explanation.\n- C. intracranial hemorrhage — a recognized cause of early seizures.\n- D. cerebral anomaly — recognized cause of early seizures.\n- E. hypoglycemia — recognized cause, usually later than the delivery room.\n\nSource explanation:\n18.(B). Seizures beginning in the delivery room or shortly thereafter may be due to the\nunintentional injection of maternal local anesthetic into the fetus. Convulsions may\nalso result from hyponatremia and water intoxication in the infant after the\nadministration of large amounts of hypotonic fluid to the mother shortly before and\nduring delivery.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Text is intact. Multiple defensible answers, and the key conflicts with the course teaching that HIE is the leading cause. Key preserved; Answer Verification Required. Decision needed on whether to accept with a modified key or exclude.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q18, PDF p.166; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":18,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-019","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["abscence of oral-buccal-lingual movements","time-synchronized electroencephalographic discharges","significant cortical epileptic activity","good response to anticonvulsant therapy","a poor prognosis"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: after severe birth asphyxia, infants may show motor automatisms: oral-buccal-lingual movements, rotary limb activity (rowing, pedaling, swimming), tonic posturing or myoclonus. These movements are usually **not accompanied by time-synchronized EEG discharges**, may not indicate cortical epileptic activity, **respond poorly to anticonvulsants** and are associated with a **poor prognosis**.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. absence of oral-buccal-lingual movements — contradicts the source (these movements are typical).\n\n- B. time-synchronized EEG discharges — contradicts the source (usually not accompanied).\n- C. significant cortical epileptic activity — contradicts the source (may not signify cortical epileptic activity).\n- D. good response to anticonvulsant therapy — contradicts the source (poor response).\n- E. a poor prognosis — consistent with the source and with the key.\n\nSource explanation:\n19.(E). After severe birth asphyxia, infants may have motor automatisms characterized\nby oral-buccal-lingual movements, rotary limb activities (rowing, pedaling, and\nswimming), tonic posturing, or myoclonus. These motor activities are not usually\naccompanied by time-synchronized electroencephalographic discharges, may not\nsignify cortical epileptic activity, respond poorly to anticonvulsant therapy, and are\nassociated with a poor prognosis. Such automatisms may represent cortical depression\nthat produces a brainstem release phenomenon or subcortical seizures.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing. The option analysis above assumes a 'true statement' stem and is not a reconstruction of it. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q19, PDF p.166; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":19,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-022","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Regarding intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) of prematurity, the following are true EXCEPT","o":["it usually develops spontaneously","MRI is the preferred imaging technique for screening IVH","severe IVH","majority of patients with IVH have no clinical symptoms","it may rarely manifest at birth"],"k":1,"ex":"IVH of prematurity is bleeding from the fragile germinal matrix in infants born before about 32 weeks. Nelson's explanation: **ultrasonography is the preferred imaging technique for screening** because it is noninvasive, portable, reproducible, and sensitive and specific.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. it usually develops spontaneously — readable; no source text addresses it directly; the hemorrhage typically occurs in the first 3 days (see Q82).\n\n- B. MRI is the preferred imaging technique for screening IVH — false per Nelson (ultrasound); matches the key.\n- C. 'severe IVH' — truncated; cannot be assessed.\n- D. majority of patients with IVH have no clinical symptoms — true.\n- E. it may rarely manifest at birth — true.\n\nSource explanation:\n22.(B). Ultrasonography is the preferred imaging technique for screening because it is\nnoninvasive, portable, reproducible, and sensitive and specific for detection of IVH.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Option C is truncated and the question number was lost in the OCR (the stem line begins without its number). The key is supported by the source explanation, but the item is incomplete. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q22, PDF pp.166-167; answer on PDF p.180","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":22,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-023","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["metabolic derangements","poor growth","increased risk for sepsis","increased risk of spontaneous bowel perforation","cerebral palsy"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: infants exposed to postnatal steroids after the first week of life have an **increased risk of cerebral palsy and developmental delay**. This is why late postnatal dexamethasone is restricted in preterm infants with BPD.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. metabolic derangements — recognized steroid effect (e.g. hyperglycemia).\n\n- B. poor growth — recognized steroid effect.\n- C. increased risk for sepsis — recognized steroid effect.\n- D. increased risk of spontaneous bowel perforation — recognized steroid effect.\n- E. cerebral palsy — stated explicitly in Nelson's explanation as increased by postnatal steroids after the first week.\n\nSource explanation:\n23.(E). Infants exposed to postnatal steroids after the 1st wk of life have an increased\nrisk of cerebral palsy and developmental delay.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; labels C and D lost in OCR. It is impossible to tell whether the item is 'EXCEPT' or not, and the key cannot be checked. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q23, PDF p.167; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":23,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-026","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The following are true regarding respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) ExCEPT Asigns of RDS usually appear within minutes of birth","o":["in most cases, the symptoms and signs reach a peak within 7days","apnea and irregular respirations are ominous signs improvement is often heralded by spontaneous diuresis"],"k":1,"ex":"RDS is caused by surfactant deficiency, with alveolar collapse and hypoxemia. Nelson's explanation states that symptoms and signs reach a peak **within 3 days**, after which improvement is gradual.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. signs of RDS usually appear within minutes of birth — true.\n\n- B. peak within 7 days — false per Nelson (3 days); matches the key.\n- C. apnea and irregular respirations are ominous signs — true.\n- D. improvement is often heralded by spontaneous diuresis — true (label lost in OCR, read by sequence).\n- E. missing from the OCR; not analyzed.\n\nSource explanation:\n26.(B). In most cases, the symptoms and signs reach a peak within 3 days, after which\nimprovement is gradual.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Option E is missing. B is false and is the keyed answer, but if E were also a false statement the item would have two exceptions. Verify option E in the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q26, PDF p.167; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":26,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-027","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["persistent pulmonary hypertension","meconium aspiration syndrome","pulmonary lymphangiectasia","lobar emphysema"],"k":null,"ex":"The source explanation for this item says that **early-onset sepsis may be indistinguishable from RDS**, and that congenital pneumonia can give an identical chest X-ray. Clues to sepsis are maternal group B streptococcal colonization, organisms on Gram stain of gastric or tracheal aspirate, and marked neutropenia.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. persistent pulmonary hypertension — a different cause of neonatal hypoxemia, mainly in term and post-term infants.\n\n- B. meconium aspiration syndrome — term or post-term infants with meconium-stained fluid.\n- C. missing from the OCR; the keyed answer is not readable.\n- D. pulmonary lymphangiectasia — a rare congenital cause of respiratory distress.\n- E. lobar emphysema — a rare cause.\n\nSource explanation:\n27.(C). In the differential diagnosis, early-onset sepsis may be indistinguishable from\nRDS. In pneumonia manifested at birth, the chest roentgenogram may be identical to\nthat for RDs. Maternal group B streptococcal colonization, identification of organisms\non gram staining of gastric or tracheal aspirates or a buffy coat smear, and/or the\npresence of marked neutropenia may suggest the diagnosis of early-onset sepsis.\nCyanotic heart disease (total anomalous pulmonary venous return) can also mimic RDS\nboth clinically and radiographically. Echocardiography with color-flow imaging should\nbe performed in infants who show no response to surfactant replacement, to rule out\ncyanotic congenital heart disease as well as ascertain patency of the ductus arteriosus\nand assess pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem and option C are missing. The question is unusable until the PDF is checked; whether the stem asks for the best differential or an exception is unknown.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q27, PDF p.167; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":27,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-028","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Prevention","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["incidence and mortality of RDS",".postnatal growth","the overall neonatal mortality","need for and duration of ventilatory support","incidence of severe intraventricular hemorrhage"],"k":null,"ex":"Antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone or dexamethasone given to mothers at risk of preterm delivery) accelerate fetal lung maturation and surfactant production, reducing the incidence and severity of RDS, neonatal mortality, need for ventilation and severe IVH. The course sheet states they decrease mortality and morbidity by about 40%.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. incidence and mortality of RDS — reduced by antenatal steroids.\n\n- B. postnatal growth — the keyed option; the source says it is not adversely affected.\n- C. the overall neonatal mortality — reduced.\n- D. need for and duration of ventilatory support — reduced.\n- E. incidence of severe intraventricular hemorrhage — reduced.\n\nSource explanation:\n28.(B). Postnatal growth is not adversely affected. Antenatal steroids do not increase\nthe risk of maternal death, chorioamnionitis, or puerperal sepsis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; label B lost in OCR. Whether the item lists effects 'except' one cannot be confirmed. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q28, PDF pp.167-168; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":28,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-033","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"following EXCEPT","o":["skin slough from infiltration","increased serum osmolarity","hypernatremia","hypocalcemia","hyperkalemia"],"k":4,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: alkali therapy given as concentrated (hypertonic) solutions through an umbilical vein catheter wedged in the liver can cause **skin slough** from infiltration, **increased serum osmolarity, hypernatremia, hypocalcemia, hypokalemia** and liver injury. Rapid hypertonic infusion also raises IVH risk in premature infants.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. skin slough from infiltration — listed.\n\n- B. increased serum osmolarity — listed.\n- C. hypernatremia — listed.\n- D. hypocalcemia — listed.\n- E. hyperkalemia — not listed; the source lists hypokalemia; matches the key.\n\nSource explanation:\n33.(E). Alkali therapy may result in skin slough from infiltration, increased serum\nosmolarity, hypernatremia, hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and liver injury when\nconcentrated solutions are administered rapidly through an umbilical vein catheter\nwedged in the liver.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing (only 'following EXCEPT' survives); the stem's subject (alkali therapy) is taken from the explanation and is not confirmed. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q33, PDF pp.168-169; answer on PDF p.181","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":33,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-034","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Include the following EXCEPT","o":["immaturity","dehydration during the 1st days of life","chorioamnionitis","symptomatic POA","malnutrition"],"k":1,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: **overhydration** in the first days of life contributes to BPD, and vitamin A supplementation (5,000 IU IM three times a week for 4 weeks) reduces the risk in VLBW infants. Other recognized risk factors are immaturity, barotrauma and volutrauma from ventilation, oxygen toxicity, chorioamnionitis (inflammation), a symptomatic PDA with fluid overload, and poor nutrition.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. immaturity — a major risk factor.\n\n- B. dehydration during the 1st days of life — opposite of the source's overhydration; matches the key.\n- C. chorioamnionitis — risk factor.\n- D. symptomatic PDA (printed 'POA' in OCR) — risk factor; the reading as PDA is an interpretation of the OCR.\n- E. malnutrition — risk factor.\n\nSource explanation:\n34.(B). Overhydration during the 1st days of life may also contribute to the\ndevelopment of BPD. Vitamin A supplementation (5,000 IU intramuscularly 3 times/wk\nfor 4 wk) in VlBW infants reduces the risk of BPD.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing (only 'Include the following EXCEPT' survives); the OCR reads PDA as 'POA'. The question is not reconstructed. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q34, PDF p.169; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":34,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-038","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"newborn (PPHN) EXCEPT","o":["anemia",".meconium aspiration syndrome","early-onset sepsis","hypoglycemia","birth asphyxia"],"k":0,"ex":"PPHN is failure of the pulmonary vascular resistance to fall after birth, producing right-to-left shunting through the ductus and foramen ovale. Nelson lists predisposing factors: birth asphyxia, meconium aspiration, early-onset sepsis, RDS, hypoglycemia, **polycythemia**, maternal NSAID use (in-utero ductal constriction), late-trimester SSRI use, and pulmonary hypoplasia from diaphragmatic hernia, oligohydramnios or pleural effusions.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. anemia — not listed (polycythemia is); matches the key.\n\n- B. meconium aspiration syndrome — listed (label lost in OCR).\n- C. early-onset sepsis — listed.\n- D. hypoglycemia — listed.\n- E. birth asphyxia — listed.\n\nSource explanation:\n38.(A). Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) occurs in term and\npost-term infants. Predisposing factors include birth asphyxia, MAS, early-onset sepsis,\nRDS, hypoglycemia, polycythemia, maternal use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory\ndrugs with in utero constriction of the ductus arteriosus, maternal late trimester use of\nselective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, and pulmonary hypoplasia due to\ndiaphragmatic hernia, amniotic fluid leak, oligohydramnios, or pleural effusions. PPHN\nis often idiopathic.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing; the most likely wording ('predisposing factors for') is not reconstructed. The key is well supported, so this is a strong candidate for acceptance once the stem is verified.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q38, PDF p.169; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":38,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-039","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["pulmonary hypoplasia","CNS lesions","esophageal atresia","omphalocele","cardiovascular lesions"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: **pulmonary hypoplasia and malrotation of the intestine are part of the lesion**, not associated anomalies. This identifies the entity as congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH).","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. pulmonary hypoplasia — part of the lesion, not an associated anomaly (the key).\n\n- B. CNS lesions — printed without a label in OCR; an associated anomaly.\n- C. esophageal atresia — associated anomaly.\n- D. omphalocele — associated anomaly.\n- E. cardiovascular lesions — associated anomaly.\n\nSource explanation:\n39.(A). Pulmonary hypoplasia and malrotation of the intestine are part of the lesion,\nnot associated anomalies.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; the item's subject (CDH) is taken from the explanation text, not from the stem. Option B's label is missing and options are split across a page break. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q39, PDF pp.169-170; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":39,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-044","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["positive result of abdominal paracentesis","failure of medical management","a single fixed bowel loop on radiographs","abdominal wall erythema","a palpable mass"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: a surgeon should be consulted early. **Absolute indications for surgery** are perforation (pneumoperitoneum on abdominal X-ray) and a **positive abdominal paracentesis** (stool or organisms on Gram stain). Failure of medical management, a single fixed bowel loop on radiographs, abdominal wall erythema and a palpable mass are **relative** indications for exploratory laparotomy.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. positive result of abdominal paracentesis — absolute indication (the key).\n\n- B. failure of medical management — relative indication.\n- C. a single fixed bowel loop on radiographs — relative indication.\n- D. abdominal wall erythema — relative indication.\n- E. a palpable mass — relative indication.\n\nSource explanation:\n44.(A). A surgeon should be consulted early in the course of treatment. Indications for\nsurgery include evidence of perforation on abdominal roentgenograms\n(pneumoperitoneum) or positive result of abdominal paracentesis (stool or organism\non Gram stain preparation from peritoneal fluid). Failure of medical management, a\nsingle fixed bowel loop on radiographs, abdominal wall erythema, and a palpable mass\nare relative indications for exploratory laparotomy.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; the key is well supported by the explanation if the stem asks for the absolute indication. The wording was not reconstructed. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q44, PDF pp.170-171; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":44,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-045","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"EXCEPT","o":["gestational age ≥41 wk",". male gender","exclusive bottle-feeding","black race","discharge from hospital after 72 hr"],"k":1,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: **male gender is a minor risk factor**. The options match the way risk factors for severe neonatal hyperbilirubinemia are usually organized (for example in the AAP 2004 guideline): the factors gestational age 41 weeks or more, exclusive bottle-feeding, black race and discharge after 72 hours are associated with **decreased** risk, whereas male gender is a minor **increased**-risk factor.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. gestational age ≥41 wk — decreased risk in the AAP grouping.\n\n- B. male gender — minor increased-risk factor per Nelson; the keyed option.\n- C. exclusive bottle-feeding — decreased risk (exclusive breast-feeding is the risk factor).\n- D. black race — decreased risk in the AAP grouping.\n- E. discharge from hospital after 72 hr — decreased risk (early discharge is the risk factor).\n\nSource explanation:\n45.(B). Male gender is a minor risk factor.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing (only 'EXCEPT' survives); line order shifted by a page break. The 'decreased risk' reading is an inference. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q45, PDF p.171; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":45,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-046","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"4 Jaundice, consisting of either indirect or direct bilirubin that is present at birth or","o":["erythroblastosis fetalis","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","concealed hemorrhage","sepsis","congenital infections"],"k":1,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: jaundice first appearing on the 2nd or 3rd day is usually physiologic, though it may represent a more severe form; Crigler-Najjar syndrome (familial nonhemolytic icterus) and early-onset breast-feeding jaundice first appear on the 2nd or 3rd day. By contrast, jaundice present at birth or in the first 24 hours is classically due to hemolysis (erythroblastosis fetalis), concealed hemorrhage, sepsis, or congenital infections (syphilis, CMV, rubella, toxoplasmosis).","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. erythroblastosis fetalis — hemolytic disease; typical cause of jaundice in the first 24 hours.\n\n- B. Crigler-Najjar syndrome — appears on days 2-3 per the source; the keyed option.\n- C. concealed hemorrhage — a recognized cause of early jaundice.\n- D. sepsis — recognized cause of early jaundice.\n- E. congenital infections — recognized cause of early jaundice.\n\nSource explanation:\n46.(B). Jaundice that first appears on the 2nd or 3rd day is usually physiologic but may\nrepresent a more severe form. Familial nonhemolytic icterus (Crigler-Najjar syndrome)\nand early-onset breast-feeding jaundice are seen initially on the 2nd or 3rd day.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem truncated and the question number is lost ('4 Jaundice, ...'); the end of the stem decides the answer. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q46, PDF p.171; answer on PDF p.182","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":46,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-049","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment","q":"4 The therapeutic effect of phototherapy depends on the following factors EXCEPT","o":["distance between the lights and the infant","skin color","surface area of exposed skin","rate of hemolysis"],"k":1,"ex":"The therapeutic effect of phototherapy depends on the **spectrum of the light** (blue, about 460-490 nm), the **irradiance** (which falls with distance between lights and infant), the **surface area of exposed skin** and the **rate of bilirubin production**, for example the rate of hemolysis. Nelson's explanation: **dark skin does not reduce efficacy** of phototherapy.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. distance between the lights and the infant — matters (irradiance).\n\n- B. skin color — does not reduce efficacy per Nelson; the keyed option.\n- C. surface area of exposed skin — matters.\n- D. rate of hemolysis — matters (bilirubin production rate).\n- E. missing from the OCR; not analyzed.\n\nSource explanation:\n49.(B). Dark skin does not reduce the efficacy of phototherapy.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Option E is missing, and the question number is lost (it starts '4 The therapeutic effect ...'). The key is well supported, but the item is incomplete. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q49, PDF p.171; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":49,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-050","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["double-bowel wall sign (bowel edema)","polyhydramnios","ascites","pleural effusions","scalp edema"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: the **early** ultrasonographic signs of hydrops are organomegaly (liver, spleen, heart), the **double-bowel wall sign** (bowel edema) and placental thickening. Progression to polyhydramnios, ascites, pleural or pericardial effusions and skin or scalp edema follows later.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. double-bowel wall sign (bowel edema) — an early sign per the source; the keyed option.\n\n- B. polyhydramnios — later progression.\n- C. ascites — later progression.\n- D. pleural effusions — later progression.\n- E. scalp edema — later progression.\n\nSource explanation:\n50.(A). Early ultrasonographic signs of hydrops include organomegaly (liver, spleen,\nheart), the double-bowel wall sign (bowel edema), and placental thickening.\nProgression to polyhydramnios, ascites, pleural or pericardial effusions, and skin or\nscalp edema may then follow.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; options split across a page break. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q50, PDF pp.171-172; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":50,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-054","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"One of the following is FALsE in plethora in the newborn infant (polycythemia)","o":["polycythemia is defined as a central Hct of 65% or higher","central values","infants","many affected infants are asymptomatic","to stasis in the microcirculation"],"k":1,"ex":"Polycythemia of the newborn is defined as a **central venous hematocrit of 65% or higher** (the course sheet agrees). Hyperviscosity and stasis in the microcirculation explain the symptoms: plethora, hypoglycemia, jaundice, respiratory distress, irritability.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. polycythemia is defined as a central Hct of 65% or higher — correct definition.\n\n- B. truncated ('central values'); likely about peripheral versus central hematocrit; the keyed option.\n- C. truncated ('infants'); cannot be analyzed.\n- D. many affected infants are asymptomatic — true.\n- E. truncated ('to stasis in the microcirculation'); cannot be analyzed.\n\nSource explanation:\n54.(B). Peripheral (heelstick) Hct values are higher than central values, whereas Coulter\ncounter results are lower than Hct values determined by microcentrifugation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Options B, C and E are truncated; the key cannot be checked. Verify against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q54, PDF p.172; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":54,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-055","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Prevention","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["age of onset is usually in 2-7 days old infants","bleeding time is normal","treatment of the disease"],"k":null,"ex":"Only fragments survive: 'age of onset is usually in 2-7 days old infants', 'bleeding time is normal' and 'treatment of the disease', which match classic vitamin K deficiency bleeding. The source explanation (key D) says oral vitamin K (1-2 mg at birth, discharge and 3-4 weeks) is less effective in preventing **late-onset** bleeding, so the **intramuscular route remains the method of choice**.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. 'age of onset is usually in 2-7 days old infants' — consistent with the classic form.\n\n- C. 'bleeding time is normal' — consistent with the disease (platelet function is normal).\n- 'treatment of the disease' — fragment; label and full wording lost.\n- Option D (the key) and the stem are missing.\n\nSource explanation:\n55.(D). Although oral vitamin K (birth, discharge, 3-4 wk: 1-2 mg) has been suggested as\nan alternative, oral vitamin K is less effective in preventing the late onset of bleeding\ndue to vitamin K deficiency and thus cannot be recommended for routine therapy. The\nintramuscular route remains the method of choice.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem and most options are missing; the question cannot be used until the PDF is checked.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q55, PDF pp.172-173; answer on PDF p.183","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":55,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-066","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["group 8 streptococci GBS","coagulase-negative staphylococcl","proteus","gonococci","chlamydiae"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: agents that commonly cause **nosocomial** neonatal infection are coagulase-negative staphylococci, gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas and others), enterococci, S. aureus and Candida. The course sheet adds that in hospital, coagulase-negative staphylococci are the typical infection in preterm infants, with invasive procedures such as umbilical catheters and ventilation as risk factors.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. group B streptococci — classic early-onset sepsis pathogen (label lost in OCR).\n\n- B. coagulase-negative staphylococci — common nosocomial pathogen; the keyed option.\n- C. proteus — a gram-negative organism; not in the source's list.\n- D. gonococci — perinatal conjunctivitis; not a typical nosocomial pathogen.\n- E. chlamydiae — perinatal conjunctivitis and pneumonia.\n\nSource explanation:\n66.(B). Agents that commonly cause nosocomial infection are coagulase-negative\nstaphylococci, gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Salmonella,\nEnterobacter, Citrobacter, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Serratia), enterococci, S. aureus,\nand Candida. Viruses contributing to nosocomial neonatal infection indude\nenteroviruses, CMv, hepatitis A, adenoviruses, influenza, respiratory syncytial virus\n(RsV), rhinovirus, parainfluenza, HSV, and rotavirus. Community-acquired pathogens\nsuch as Streptococcus pneumoniae may also cause infection in newborn infants after\ndischarge from the hospital","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; option B label lost. The key is consistent with the explanation only if the stem asks about nosocomial pathogens. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q66, PDF p.175; answer on PDF p.184","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":66,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-071","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["cytomegalovirus","herpes simplex virus","Cvaricella-zoster virus","HIV","rubella"],"k":null,"ex":"The key is C (varicella-zoster virus), but the stem is missing and the source explanation contains only the key, so no source text supports it. In general: CMV causes periventricular calcification, microcephaly and hearing loss; HSV causes vesicles, keratoconjunctivitis and encephalitis; **congenital varicella** causes dermatomal skin scarring, limb hypoplasia, microcephaly and eye damage; HIV is usually asymptomatic at birth; rubella causes cataract, deafness and PDA.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. cytomegalovirus — periventricular calcification, microcephaly, hearing loss.\n\n- B. herpes simplex virus — skin vesicles, encephalitis, keratoconjunctivitis.\n- C. varicella-zoster virus — the keyed option; dermatomal scarring and limb hypoplasia in the congenital syndrome.\n- D. HIV — usually asymptomatic at birth.\n- E. rubella — cataract, deafness, congenital heart disease (PDA).\n\nSource explanation:\n71.(C).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing and the source explanation has no text. Key unverifiable. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q71, PDF pp.175-176; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":71,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-073","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"7Common skin and soft tissue manifestations of neonatal bacterial infections are","o":["facial cellulitis","scalp abscess","fasciitis","breast abscess","omphalitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Neonatal skin and soft tissue infections include omphalitis, impetigo (bullous), scalp abscess (often at electrode or monitoring sites), facial cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis and mastitis or breast abscess. The source explanation says breast abscess and impetigo are the common ones, and the key is D (breast abscess).","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. facial cellulitis — recognized, less common.\n\n- B. scalp abscess — recognized.\n- C. fasciitis — recognized but uncommon (necrotizing fasciitis).\n- D. breast abscess — named in the source as common; the keyed option.\n- E. omphalitis — a very common neonatal skin and soft tissue infection; competing defensible answer.\n\nSource explanation:\n73.(D). Breast abscess and impetigo are common skin and soft tissue manifestations of\nneonatal bacterial infections","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Text intact apart from a merged question number ('7Common'). Multiple defensible answers. Key preserved; Answer Verification Required.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q73, PDF p.176; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":73,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-074","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other","q":"One of the foilowing is not included in the definition of the systemic inflammatory response (SiRS) in neonates and pediatric patients","o":["temperature instability","abnormal white blood cell (wBC) count","Crespiratory dysfunction","cardiac dysfunction","perfusion abnormalities"],"k":1,"ex":"The key B says an abnormal white blood cell count is the item not included in the SIRS definition in neonates and children. Nelson's explanation says abnormal WBC count or increased immature forms is included in the definition of SIRS **in adults**.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. temperature instability — a SIRS criterion.\n\n- B. abnormal white blood cell count — a SIRS criterion in the pediatric consensus; the keyed option (conflict).\n- C. respiratory dysfunction — organ dysfunction rather than a SIRS criterion; defensible as the exception.\n- D. cardiac dysfunction — organ dysfunction; defensible as the exception.\n- E. perfusion abnormalities — organ dysfunction; defensible as the exception.\n\nSource explanation:\n74.(B). Abnormal white blood cell (WBC) count or an increase in immature forms is\nincluded in the definition of the systemic inflammatory response SiRS in adults.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Genuine answer-key conflict with the pediatric consensus. Key preserved; Answer Verification Required. Decision needed.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q74, PDF p.176; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":74,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-077","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"infants is","o":["delivery by cesarean section","breast feeding","apgar score","exposure to glucocorticoids during the first week of life","gestational age and birth weight"],"k":4,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: in an analysis of 15,072 neonates born at 98 centers over 2 years, the most important variables associated with NEC were **gestational age and birth weight**, i.e., prematurity. Prematurity is the greatest risk factor for NEC, with immature gut motility and barrier function, enteral feeding and ischemia.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. delivery by cesarean section — not identified by the source as a major variable.\n\n- B. breast feeding — protective against NEC.\n- C. Apgar score — not identified by the source as a major variable.\n- D. exposure to glucocorticoids during the first week of life — not identified as the major variable.\n- E. gestational age and birth weight — the most important variables in the cited analysis; the keyed option.\n\nSource explanation:\n77.(E). In an analysis of 15,072 neonates born at 98 centers over a 2-year period, the\nmost important variables associated with NEC were gestational age and birth weight.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing. The key is well supported by the explanation. Verify the stem against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q77, PDF p.177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":77,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-078","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"78Pneumatosis intestinalis is pathognomonic for","o":["Hirschsprung's disease","necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC)","pseudomembranous enterocolitis","neonatal ulcerative colitis","meconium ileus"],"k":4,"ex":"Pneumatosis intestinalis is gas within the bowel wall, produced by bacterial fermentation (hydrogen) and seen on abdominal X-ray as bubbly or linear dark rings. Nelson's explanation: it can be seen in other conditions (Hirschsprung disease, pseudomembranous enterocolitis, neonatal ulcerative colitis, ischemic bowel), but it is a **characteristic finding in about 85% of NEC**.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. Hirschsprung's disease — pneumatosis can occur with Hirschsprung enterocolitis, but is not characteristic.\n\n- B. necrotizing enterocolitis — the usual answer; the source explanation names NEC as the characteristic setting.\n- C. pseudomembranous enterocolitis — can show pneumatosis occasionally.\n- D. neonatal ulcerative colitis — can show pneumatosis occasionally.\n- E. meconium ileus — the keyed option; contradicts the source explanation.\n\nSource explanation:\n78.(E). Pneumatosis intestinalis can be seen in various other conditions, including\nHirschsprung's disease, pseudomembranous enterocolitis, neonatal ulcerative colitis,\nand ischemic bowel disease. However, it is a characteristic finding in 85% of patients\nwith NEC. Dark, concentric rings within the bowel wall represent hydrogen as a\nbyproduct of bacterial metabolism","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Genuine key conflict: the key contradicts the book's own explanation. Key preserved; Answer Verification Required. Likely a key or OCR error, to be confirmed against the PDF.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q78, PDF p.177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":78,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-081","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Question stem unavailable in the supplied OCR.","o":["jitteriness","seizures","Ccarpopedal spasm",".high-pitched cry","laryngospasm"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: **Chvostek's sign** (facial twitching on tapping) and **Trousseau's sign** (carpopedal spasm) may be present in hypocalcemia, but more commonly they are **absent** in the neonatal period. The course sheet describes neonatal hypocalcemia as presenting with jitteriness and convulsions, usually on the 2nd day, treated with IV calcium gluconate.","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. jitteriness — typical.\n\n- B. seizures — typical.\n- C. carpopedal spasm — the keyed option; uncommon in neonates per the source.\n- D. high-pitched cry — typical (label lost in OCR).\n- E. laryngospasm — recognized sign.\n\nSource explanation:\n81.(C). Chvostek's sign (facial muscle twitching on tapping), and Trousseau's sign\n(carpopedal spasm) may be present, but more commonly these are absent during the\nneonatal period.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem missing; labels partly lost. The key is consistent with the explanation. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q81, PDF p.177; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":81,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEONREVIEW-082","spec":"neonatology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"ultrasound is first day of life second day of life","o":["third day of life","fourth day of life","fifth day of life"],"k":null,"ex":"Nelson's explanation: in infants studied by ultrasonography, about **50% of IVH began on the first day of life, 25% on the second and 15% on the third**, so a single scan on the **fourth day** would be expected to detect more than 90% of IVH. This supports D (fourth day).","more":"Options analysis:\n- A. first day of life — about 50% of IVH begins here, but later hemorrhages would be missed.\n\n- B. second day of life — label lost in OCR.\n- C. third day of life.\n- D. fourth day of life — the keyed option; would detect more than 90% per the source.\n- E. fifth day of life.\n\nSource explanation:\n82.(D). In a series of infants studied by ultrasonography, approximately 50% had the\nonset of hemorrhage on the first day of life, 25% on the second day, and 15% on the\nthird day. Thus, a single scan on the fourth day of life would be expected to detect\n90% of IVHs.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Needs Review","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stem start missing; labels A and B lost. The key is consistent with the explanation. Verify against the PDF.\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs Q82, PDF pp.177-178; answer on PDF p.185","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Neonatology","nlNumber":82,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-001","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The part of the glomerulus which serves as a supporting and probably has a role in the regulation of glomerular blood flow, filtration, and removal of macromolecules is the","o":["glomerular basement membrane","mesangium","bowman's capsule","parietal epithelial cell","visceral epithelial"],"k":1,"ex":"The source key identifies option B (mesangium) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. glomerular basement membrane\n\n - B. mesangium\n - C. bowman's capsule\n - D. parietal epithelial cell\n - E. visceral epithelial","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.401 (printed p.391, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-002","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Collectively, the glomerular endothelial cell, basement membrane, and podocytes form the","o":["glomerulus","filtration membrane","juxtameduilary nephron","nephron","renal corpuscle *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*"],"k":1,"ex":"The source key identifies option B (filtration membrane) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. glomerulus\n\n - B. filtration membrane\n - C. juxtameduilary nephron\n - D. nephron\n - E. renal corpuscle *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.401 (printed p.391, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-003","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The ultra-filtrate which is filtered through the glomerular capillary walls contains all of the following EXCEPT","o":["electrolytes","low-molecular-weight proteins","creatinine","albumin","glucose"],"k":3,"ex":"The ultra-filtrate, which is cell free, contains all of the substances in plasma (electrolytes, glucose, phosphate, urea, creatinine, peptides, and low-molecular-weight proteins except proteins having a molecular weight of z68 kDa [such as albumin and globulins]).","more":"Options:\n- A. electrolytes\n\n - B. low-molecular-weight proteins\n - C. creatinine\n - D. albumin\n - E. glucose","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.401 (printed p.391, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-008","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Glomerular hematuria is associated with","o":["urinary microscopic findings of RBC casts","minimal proteinuria on dipstick (<100 mg/dL)","gross hematuria that is bright red or pink","the presence of leukocytes cast","normal urinary RBC morphology"],"k":0,"ex":"Hematuria from within the glomerulus is often associated with urinary microscopic findings of RBC casts, and deformed urinary RBCs (particularly acanthocytes), brown, cola- or tea-colored, or burgundy urine, and proteinuria >100 mg/dLvia dipstick. Hematuria originating within the tubular system may be associated with the presence of leukocytes or renal tubular casts.","more":"Options:\n- A. urinary microscopic findings of RBC casts\n\n - B. minimal proteinuria on dipstick (<100 mg/dL)\n - C. gross hematuria that is bright red or pink\n - D. the presence of leukocytes cast\n - E. normal urinary RBC morphology","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.402 (printed p.392, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-009","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In hematuria, renal biopsy is indicated in all the following ExCEPT","o":["some children with persistent microscopic hematuria","decreased renal function","proteinuria","hypertension","persistent high serum complement C3"],"k":4,"ex":"Renal biopsy is indicated for children with persistent low serum complement C3","more":"Options:\n- A. some children with persistent microscopic hematuria\n\n - B. decreased renal function\n - C. proteinuria\n - D. hypertension\n - E. persistent high serum complement C3","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.402 (printed p.392, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":9,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-010","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"IgA nephropathy is characterized by","o":["hematuria occur after skin infection","proteinuria is often >1000 mg/24 hr","severe hypertension","normal serum levels of C3","serum IgA levels have a diagnostic value"],"k":3,"ex":"Gross hematuria often occurs within 1-2 days of onset of an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection, in contrast to the longer latency period observed in acute post infectious glomerulonephritis, and may be associated with loin pain. Proteinuria is often <1000 mg/24 hr in patients with asymptomatic microscopic hematuria.","more":"Options:\n- A. hematuria occur after skin infection\n\n - B. proteinuria is often >1000 mg/24 hr\n - C. severe hypertension\n - D. normal serum levels of C3\n - E. serum IgA levels have a diagnostic value","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.402 (printed p.392, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.419","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-013","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Alport Syndrome AS, is a genetically heterogeneous disease caused by mutations in the genes coding for type IV collagen, it is characterized by","o":["asymptomatic microscopic hematuria","proteinuria<1 g/24 hr.","congenital sensor neural hearing loss","ocular abnormalities in 80% of X-linked type","leiomyomatosis of the esophagus is common"],"k":0,"ex":"All patients with AS have asymptomatic microscopic hematuria, which may be intermittent in girls and younger boys. Progressive proteinuria, often exceeding 1 g/24 hr., is common by the 2nd decade of life and can be severe enough to cause nephrotic syndrome.","more":"Options:\n- A. asymptomatic microscopic hematuria\n\n - B. proteinuria<1 g/24 hr.\n - C. congenital sensor neural hearing loss\n - D. ocular abnormalities in 80% of X-linked type\n - E. leiomyomatosis of the esophagus is common","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.403 (printed p.393, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.420","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-016","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The best single test to document cutaneous streptococcal infection is","o":["low serum C3 level","elevated antistreptolysin O titer","positive streptozyme screen","antideoxyribonuclease B level","depressed serum CH50"],"k":3,"ex":"Four differences in APSGN follow throat or skin infections: Post streptococcal GN commonly follows streptococcal pharyngitis during cold-weather months and streptococcal skin infections or pyoderma during warm-weather months. Epidemics of nephritis have been described in association with throat (serotypes M1, M4, M25, and some strains of M12) and skin (serotype M49) infections.","more":"Options:\n- A. low serum C3 level\n\n - B. elevated antistreptolysin O titer\n - C. positive streptozyme screen\n - D. antideoxyribonuclease B level\n - E. depressed serum CH50","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.404 (printed p.394, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.420","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":16,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-018","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Renal biopsy in acute post streptococcal glomerulonephritis should be considered in all the following EXCEPT","o":["acute renal failure","nephrotic syndrome","absence of evidence of streptococcal infection","low C3 level in the first 2 months","initially normal complement levels"],"k":3,"ex":"Renal biopsy is considered when hematuria and proteinuria, diminished renal function, and/or a low C3 level persist more than 2 mo. after onset.","more":"Options:\n- A. acute renal failure\n\n - B. nephrotic syndrome\n - C. absence of evidence of streptococcal infection\n - D. low C3 level in the first 2 months\n - E. initially normal complement levels","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.404 (printed p.394, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.421","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":18,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-021","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The well-known complication of membranous nephropathy is","o":["renal vein thrombosis","hypertension 2%","hematuria acute renal failure","gross hematuria","nephritic syndrome"],"k":0,"ex":"A subset of patients with MN present with a major venous thrombosis, commonly renal vein thrombosis, this well-known complication. Approximately 20% of children have hypertension at presentation, most patients also have microscopic hematuria and only rarely present with gross hematuria.","more":"Options:\n- A. renal vein thrombosis\n\n - B. hypertension 2%\n - C. hematuria acute renal failure\n - D. gross hematuria\n - E. nephritic syndrome","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.405 (printed p.395, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.421","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":21,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-022","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Type I membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is","o":["less common than type II membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","not to be mediated by immune complexes","associated with partial lipodystrophy","called dense deposit disease","characterized by low C3 complement level"],"k":4,"ex":"Type II MPGN (dense deposit disease) is less common than type 1 membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, not to be mediated by immune complexes, demonstrate an associated systemic disease called partial lipodystrophy, serum C3 complement levels are low in the majority of cases(while in membranous nephropathy is normal), Patients present in equal proportions with nephrotic syndrome, acute nephritic syndrome, or persistent asymptomatic microscopic hematuria and proteinuria (while in membranous nephropathy usually manifest as nephrotic syndrome).","more":"Options:\n- A. less common than type II membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis\n\n - B. not to be mediated by immune complexes\n - C. associated with partial lipodystrophy\n - D. called dense deposit disease\n - E. characterized by low C3 complement level","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.405 (printed p.395, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.421","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":22,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-029","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hemolytic-uremic syndrome is a common cause of community acquired acute kidney injury in young children; atypical type may be triggered by","o":["toxin-producing E-coli","raw milk","Streptococcus pneumoniae","shiga toxin","cyclosporine"],"k":4,"ex":"The source key identifies option E (cyclosporine) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. toxin-producing E-coli\n\n - B. raw milk\n - C. Streptococcus pneumoniae\n - D. shiga toxin\n - E. cyclosporine","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.406 (printed p.396, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.422","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":29,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-031","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS). Of the following, the MOsTcorrect statement is","o":["majority of patients of diarrhea-associated enteropathictype develops HUS","stool culture is often positive in patients who have diarrhea-associated HUS","kidney biopsy is rarely indicated to diagnose HUS","partial thromboplastin and prothrombin times are usually low","Coombs test is usually negative in in pneumococci-induced HUS"],"k":2,"ex":"Kidney biopsies are only rarely performed in HUS because the diagnosis is usually established by clinical criteria and the risks of biopsy are significant during the active phase of the disease. The organisms that cause HUS may be rapidly cleared, therefore, the stool culture is often negative in patients who have diarrhea-associated HUs, Partial thromboplastin and prothrombin times are usually normal, the Coombs test is negative, with the exception of pneumococci-induced HUS, where the Coombs test is usually positive.","more":"Options:\n- A. majority of patients of diarrhea-associated enteropathictype develops HUS\n\n - B. stool culture is often positive in patients who have diarrhea-associated HUS\n - C. kidney biopsy is rarely indicated to diagnose HUS\n - D. partial thromboplastin and prothrombin times are usually low\n - E. Coombs test is usually negative in in pneumococci-induced HUS","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF pp.406–407 (printed p.396, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.422","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-032","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In the treatment of diarrhea-associated hemolytic-uremic syndrome. One of the following options has no beneficial role","o":["dialysis","early intravenous volume expansion","control of hypertension","eculizumab","red cell transfusions"],"k":3,"ex":"Eculizumab is an anti-C5 antibody that inhibits complement activation, a pathway that contributes to active disease in some forms of atypical familial HUS, Eculizumab is FDA approved for the treatment of atypical HUS, while initial reports suggested that eculizumab provided benefit in patients with diarrhea associated HUS, subsequent systematic analysis showed no benefit from either plasma exchange or eculizumab.","more":"Options:\n- A. dialysis\n\n - B. early intravenous volume expansion\n - C. control of hypertension\n - D. eculizumab\n - E. red cell transfusions","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.407 (printed p.397, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.422","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-035","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following causes hematuria associated with rapid development of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia or enlargement of the kidney(s) ExCEPT","o":["nephrotic syndrome","renal vein thrombosis *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","polycystic kidney disease","Wilms tumor","hemolytic-uremic syndrome"],"k":0,"ex":"These include hemolytic uremic syndrome, hydronephrosis, polycystic kidney disease, Wilms tumor, and intrarenall abscess or hematoma. All patients should be evaluated for congenital and acquired hypercoagulable states.","more":"Options:\n- A. nephrotic syndrome\n\n - B. renal vein thrombosis *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - C. polycystic kidney disease\n - D. Wilms tumor\n - E. hemolytic-uremic syndrome","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.407 (printed p.397, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.422","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":35,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-043","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following methods offer more precise information regarding urine protein excretion?","o":["frothy urine","dipstick testing","micro albuminuria","spot urine for protein/creatinine ratio","24 hr. urine for protein and creatinine excretion"],"k":4,"ex":"The source key identifies option E (24 hr. urine for protein and creatinine excretion) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. frothy urine\n\n - B. dipstick testing\n - C. micro albuminuria\n - D. spot urine for protein/creatinine ratio\n - E. 24 hr. urine for protein and creatinine excretion","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.409 (printed p.399, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.423","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":43,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-044","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Transient proteinuria can be seen in all the following ExCEPT","o":["temperature >38.3°","exercise","over hydration","cold exposure","seizures"],"k":2,"ex":"Defined contributing factors include a temperature >38.3°C (101°F), exercise, dehydration, cold exposure, heart failure, seizures, or stress.","more":"Options:\n- A. temperature >38.3°\n\n - B. exercise\n - C. over hydration\n - D. cold exposure\n - E. seizures","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.409 (printed p.399, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.423","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":44,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-045","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Orthostatic proteinuria is increased amounts of protein in the upright position. Of the following, the MOsT appropriate answer is","o":["usually symptomatic","hematuria may be present","renal dysfunction are absent","the cause is well-known","edema usually positive"],"k":2,"ex":"The absence of proteinuria (dipstick negative or trace for protein; and a normal ratio of urinary protein [mg/dL] to urinary creatinine [mg/dL]= [uPr/uCr] <0.2) on the first morning urine sample for 3 consecutive days confirms the diagnosis of orthostatic proteinuria, children with this condition are usually asymptomatic; hematuria, hypertension, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and renal dysfunction are absent, the cause of orthostatic proteinuria is unknown, although altered renal hemodynamics and partial left renal vein…","more":"Options:\n- A. usually symptomatic\n\n - B. hematuria may be present\n - C. renal dysfunction are absent\n - D. the cause is well-known\n - E. edema usually positive","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.409 (printed p.399, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.423","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":45,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-048","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Nephrotic syndrome is a hypercoagulable state resulting from","o":["hemodilution","decreased platelet number","changes in coagulation factor levels *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","decrease in hepatic production of fibrinogen *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","high level of antithrombin III"],"k":2,"ex":"Vascular stasis from hemoconcentration,intravascular volume depletion , increased platelet number and aggregability, increase in hepatic production of fibrinogen,and low level of antithrombin Ill, cause hypercoagulable state.","more":"Options:\n- A. hemodilution\n\n - B. decreased platelet number\n - C. changes in coagulation factor levels *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - D. decrease in hepatic production of fibrinogen *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - E. high level of antithrombin III","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.410 (printed p.400, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.423","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":48,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-049","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"When you investigate for first attack of minimal change nephrotic syndrome (MCNS), one of the following is TRUE","o":["microscopic hematuria is present in 20% of children","spot urine protein : creatinine ratio should be <2.0","serum complement levels are low","renal biopsy is routinely performed","2+ proteinuria"],"k":0,"ex":"A spot urine protein : creatinine ratio should be >2.0, serum complement levels are normal, a renal biopsy is not routinely performed if the patient fits the standard clinical picture of McNS, and the urinalysis reveals 3+ or 4+ proteinuria.","more":"Options:\n- A. microscopic hematuria is present in 20% of children\n\n - B. spot urine protein : creatinine ratio should be <2.0\n - C. serum complement levels are low\n - D. renal biopsy is routinely performed\n - E. 2+ proteinuria","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.410 (printed p.400, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":49,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-052","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"To reduce the risk of serious infections in children with nephrotic syndrome, all the following immunizations can be used ExCEPT","o":["pneumococcal 13-valent conjugant vaccine","influenza vaccine annually to the child","pneumococcal 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine","varicella-zoster vaccine","influenza vaccination annually to their household contacts"],"k":3,"ex":"Following close contact with varicella infection, give immune compromised children on immunosuppressive agents varicella-zoster immune globulin if available.","more":"Options:\n- A. pneumococcal 13-valent conjugant vaccine\n\n - B. influenza vaccine annually to the child\n - C. pneumococcal 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine\n - D. varicella-zoster vaccine\n - E. influenza vaccination annually to their household contacts","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.410 (printed p.400, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":52,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-055","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The definitive treatment of primary congenital nephrotic syndrome is","o":["angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors","aggressive nutritional support","bilateral nephrectomies","renal transplantation"],"k":4,"ex":"Renal transplantation is the definitive treatment of congenital nephrotic syndrome, though recurrence of the nephrotic syndrome has been reported to occur after transplantation.","more":"Options:\n- A. angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors\n\n - B. prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors\n - C. aggressive nutritional support\n - D. bilateral nephrectomies\n - E. renal transplantation","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.411 (printed p.401, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":55,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-059","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Urinalysis in patients with Fanconi syndrome may show all the following ExCEPT","o":["urine pH is alkaline","glycosuria","uricosuria","phosphaturia","elevated urinary sodium"],"k":0,"ex":"The urine pH is acidic (<5.5) because distal acidification mechanisms are intact in these patients.","more":"Options:\n- A. urine pH is alkaline\n\n - B. glycosuria\n - C. uricosuria\n - D. phosphaturia\n - E. elevated urinary sodium","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.412 (printed p.402, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":59,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-060","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Distal RTA can be presented with","o":["hypocalciuria","hypercitraturia","phosphaturia","conductive deafness","growth failure"],"k":4,"ex":"Hypercalciuria, hypocitraturia, and sensorineural deafness are seen in distal RTA while phosphate and massive bicarbonate wasting are characteristic of proximal RTA.","more":"Options:\n- A. hypocalciuria\n\n - B. hypercitraturia\n - C. phosphaturia\n - D. conductive deafness\n - E. growth failure","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.412 (printed p.402, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":60,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-062","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Pathogenesis of hyperkalemic (Type IV) renal tubular acidosis is due to","o":["hyperaldosteronism","inhibiting ammonia genesis","pseudohyperaldosteronism","massive bicarbonate wasting","all of the above"],"k":1,"ex":"Type IV RTA occurs as the result of impaired aldosterone production (hypoaldosteronism) or impaired renal responsiveness to aldosterone (pseudohypoaldosteronism), proximal RTA pathogenesis is massive bicarbonate wasting. Acidosis results because aldosterone has a direct effect on the H+/ATPase responsible for hydrogen secretion.","more":"Options:\n- A. hyperaldosteronism\n\n - B. inhibiting ammonia genesis\n - C. pseudohyperaldosteronism\n - D. massive bicarbonate wasting\n - E. all of the above","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.412 (printed p.402, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.424","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":62,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-064","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Nephrogenic diabetes inspidus is a disorder of water metabolism characterized by an inability to concentrate urine, even in the presence of antidiuretic hormone. Of the following, the MOsT correct statement is","o":["most common inheritance is autosomal dominant","can be caused by hypokalemia","mutations in the AQP2 gene in X-linked form","irritability and crying are uncommon features","secondary form present with hyponatremia"],"k":1,"ex":"The most common pattern of inheritance in congenital NDl is an X-linked recessive disorder, mutations in the AQp2 gene have been identified in patients with the rarer autosomal dominant and recessive forms, Irritability and crying are common features, patients with the secondary form generally present later in life, primarily with hypernatremia and polyuria.","more":"Options:\n- A. most common inheritance is autosomal dominant\n\n - B. can be caused by hypokalemia\n - C. mutations in the AQP2 gene in X-linked form\n - D. irritability and crying are uncommon features\n - E. secondary form present with hyponatremia","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF pp.412–413 (printed p.402, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.425","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":64,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-066","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Bartter syndrome is a group of disorders characterized by hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with hypercalciuria and salt wasting. It can be presented with all of the following ExCEPT","o":["maternal polyhydramnios","severe episodes of recurrent dehydration","failure to thrive in classic Bartter syndrome","deafness is associated with antenatal Bartter syndrome","hypertension due to hyperaldosteronism"],"k":4,"ex":"Blood pressure is usually normal. Patients with the antenatal form can have severe salt wasting, resulting in dehydration and hypotension.","more":"Options:\n- A. maternal polyhydramnios\n\n - B. severe episodes of recurrent dehydration\n - C. failure to thrive in classic Bartter syndrome\n - D. deafness is associated with antenatal Bartter syndrome\n - E. hypertension due to hyperaldosteronism","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.413 (printed p.403, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.425","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":66,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-069","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Gitelman syndrome is a rare cause of autosomal recessive hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, the distinctive feature is","o":["Hypercalciuria","hypermagnesuria","hypernatremia","low aldosterone levels *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","elevated prostagiandin E secretion"],"k":1,"ex":"The urinary calcium level is usually very low (in contrast to the elevated urinary calcium level often seen in Bartter syndrome), hypokalemia, aldosterone levels usually normal, prostaglandin E secretion is not elevated.","more":"Options:\n- A. Hypercalciuria\n\n - B. hypermagnesuria\n - C. hypernatremia\n - D. low aldosterone levels *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - E. elevated prostagiandin E secretion","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF pp.413–414 (printed p.403, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.425","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":69,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-071","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Acute kidney injury AKl is a clinical syndrome in which a sudden deterioration in renal function results in the inability of the kidneys to maintain fluid and electrolyte homeostasis. Of the following, the MOsT correct matching is","o":["low urine sodium (UNa< 20 mEq/L) : intrinsic AKI","fractional excretion of sodium >2% : prerenal AKI","elevated urine osmolality (UOsm>500 mOsm/kg) : post renal AKI","specific gravity of <1.010 : intrinsic AKl","red blood cell casts :prerenal AKI"],"k":3,"ex":"Patients whose urine shows an elevated specific gravity (>1.020), elevated urine osmolality (U0sm>500 mOsm/kg), (ow urine sodium (UNa< 20 mEq/L), and fractional excretion of sodium <1% (<2.5% in neonates) most likely have prerenal AKl. Those with a specific gravity of <1.010, low urine osmolality (U0sm< 350 mOsm/kg), high urine sodium (UNa> 40 mEq/L), and fractional excretion of sodium >2% (>10% in neonates), red blood cell casts most likely have intrinsic AKl and low urine osmolality (UOsm< 350 mOsm/kg) in Post renal AKi.","more":"Options:\n- A. low urine sodium (UNa< 20 mEq/L) : intrinsic AKI\n\n - B. fractional excretion of sodium >2% : prerenal AKI\n - C. elevated urine osmolality (UOsm>500 mOsm/kg) : post renal AKI\n - D. specific gravity of <1.010 : intrinsic AKl\n - E. red blood cell casts :prerenal AKI","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.414 (printed p.404, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.425","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":71,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-073","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hyperkalemia can lead to cardiac arrhythmia, cardiac arrest, and death. Serum potassium can be lowered by all the following ExCEPT","o":["regular insulin with glucose","calcium gluconate","sodium bicarbonate","Kayexalate","Dialysis"],"k":1,"ex":"Calcium gluconate counteracts the potassium-induced increase in myocardial irritability but does not lower the serum potassium level.","more":"Options:\n- A. regular insulin with glucose\n\n - B. calcium gluconate\n - C. sodium bicarbonate\n - D. Kayexalate\n - E. Dialysis","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.414 (printed p.404, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.425","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":73,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-075","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Indications for dialysis in acute kidney injury include all the following ExCEPT","o":["anuria/oliguria","persistent hypercalcemia","severe metabolic acidosis","volume overload","blood urea nitrogen >100-150 mg/dl"],"k":1,"ex":"Persistent hyperkalemia.","more":"Options:\n- A. anuria/oliguria\n\n - B. persistent hypercalcemia\n - C. severe metabolic acidosis\n - D. volume overload\n - E. blood urea nitrogen >100-150 mg/dl","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF p.415 (printed p.405, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.426","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":75,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-091","spec":"nephrology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The following formulas are-true EXCEPT","o":["sodium requirement= 0.6 xweight in kg x(125- serum sodium in mEa/L)","blood anion gap=[Na] - [CI +HCO3]","blood anion gap=[Na - CI -HCO3]","urine anion gap (urine Na + urine K+] - urine Cl-)","estimated GFR = 0.43 x height in m/serum creatinine in mg/dL"],"k":4,"ex":"Schwartz formula for estimated GFR = O.43 x height in cm/ serum creatinine in mg/DI.","more":"Options:\n- A. sodium requirement= 0.6 xweight in kg x(125- serum sodium in mEa/L)\n\n - B. blood anion gap=[Na] - [CI +HCO3]\n - C. blood anion gap=[Na - CI -HCO3]\n - D. urine anion gap (urine Na + urine K+] - urine Cl-)\n - E. estimated GFR = 0.43 x height in m/serum creatinine in mg/dL","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Nephrology chapter, PDF pp.417–418 (printed p.407, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.426","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":91,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-001-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Glomerular filtration rate in full term neonate is","o":["15 mL/min/1.73 m^²","25 mL/min/1.73 m2","35mL/min/1.73 m^{2","45 mL/min/1.73 m²","55 mL/min/1.73 m^{2"],"k":1,"ex":"The source key identifies option B (25 mL/min/1.73 m2) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. 15 mL/min/1.73 m^²\n\n - B. 25 mL/min/1.73 m2\n - C. 35mL/min/1.73 m^{2\n - D. 45 mL/min/1.73 m²\n - E. 55 mL/min/1.73 m^{2","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.427 (printed p.417, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-002-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Potter syndrome (bilateral renal agenesis) is characterized by all the following EXCEPT","o":["widely separated eyes","polyhydramnios *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","low set ears","broad nose","limb anomalies"],"k":1,"ex":"Oligohydramnios.","more":"Options:\n- A. widely separated eyes\n\n - B. polyhydramnios *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - C. low set ears\n - D. broad nose\n - E. limb anomalies","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.427 (printed p.417, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-003-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Death in neonates with bilateral renal agenesis is due to","o":["renal failure","hypertension","pulmonary insufficiency","congenital heart disease *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","sepsis"],"k":2,"ex":"Death is due to pulmonary insufficiency from pulmonary hypoplasia rather than renal failure.","more":"Options:\n- A. renal failure\n\n - B. hypertension\n - C. pulmonary insufficiency\n - D. congenital heart disease *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - E. sepsis","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.427 (printed p.417, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-006","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are risk factors for urinary tract infection ExcEPT","o":["male gender","tight clothing (underwear)","pinworm infestation","poor toilet training","voiding dysfunction"],"k":0,"ex":"Female gender and uncircumcised male.","more":"Options:\n- A. male gender\n\n - B. tight clothing (underwear)\n - C. pinworm infestation\n - D. poor toilet training\n - E. voiding dysfunction","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.428 (printed p.418, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":6,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-008-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Symptoms of cystitis include","o":["fever","polyuria","nausea","suprapubic pain","urinary retention"],"k":3,"ex":"Symptoms include dysuria, urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain, incontinence, and malodorous urine. Cystitis does not cause fever and does not result in renal injury; malodorous urine is not specific for a UTI.","more":"Options:\n- A. fever\n\n - B. polyuria\n - C. nausea\n - D. suprapubic pain\n - E. urinary retention","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.428 (printed p.418, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-012","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are indications for admission to hospital in patients with urosepsis EXCEPT","o":["dehydration","unable to drink fluids","neonatal period","vomiting","microscopic hematuria"],"k":4,"ex":"Microscopic hematuria is common in acute cystitis, but micro hematuria alone does not suggest UTI.","more":"Options:\n- A. dehydration\n\n - B. unable to drink fluids\n - C. neonatal period\n - D. vomiting\n - E. microscopic hematuria","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.429 (printed p.419, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":12,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-013-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Regarding vesicoureteral reflux VUR (the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder to the ureter and kidney). All the following are true ExCEPT","o":["it is usually congenital","it may cause hypertension in children","the mean age at VUR resolution is 10yr","severity is graded according to voiding cystourethrogram","reimplantation of the ureters corrects the condition"],"k":2,"ex":"The mean age at VUR resolution is 6yr.","more":"Options:\n- A. it is usually congenital\n\n - B. it may cause hypertension in children\n - C. the mean age at VUR resolution is 10yr\n - D. severity is graded according to voiding cystourethrogram\n - E. reimplantation of the ureters corrects the condition","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.429 (printed p.419, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-015","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Regarding the voiding cystourethrogram (VcUG) for diagnosis of vesicoureteral reflux (VUR). Of the following, the MOsT appropriate statement is","o":["low-pressure VUR is occurring during voiding","high-pressure VUR is occurring during bladder filling","high-pressure VUR is significantly less likely to resolve spontaneously","contrast VCUG is significantly exposed to less radiation than radionuclide cystogram","contrast VCUG provides more anatomic information"],"k":4,"ex":"VUR occurring during bladder filling is termed low-pressure VUR; VUR during voiding is termed high-pressure VUR.VUR in children with low-pressure VUR is significantly less likely to resolve spontaneously than in children who exhibit only high- pressure VUR. Radiation exposure during a radionuclide cystogram is significantly less than that from a contrast VCUG.","more":"Options:\n- A. low-pressure VUR is occurring during voiding\n\n - B. high-pressure VUR is occurring during bladder filling\n - C. high-pressure VUR is significantly less likely to resolve spontaneously\n - D. contrast VCUG is significantly exposed to less radiation than radionuclide cystogram\n - E. contrast VCUG provides more anatomic information","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.429 (printed p.419, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.436","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":15,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-030","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Family history in nocturnal enuresis is positive in","o":["10%","30%","50%","70%","90%"],"k":2,"ex":"The source key identifies option C (50%) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. 10%\n\n - B. 30%\n - C. 50%\n - D. 70%\n - E. 90%","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.432 (printed p.422, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.437","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":30,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-031-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The pathogenesis of nocturnal enuresis is","o":["defective sleep arousal","nocturnal polyuria","genetic factors","overactive bladder","multifactorial"],"k":4,"ex":"The source key identifies option E (multifactorial) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. defective sleep arousal\n\n - B. nocturnal polyuria\n - C. genetic factors\n - D. overactive bladder\n - E. multifactorial","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.432 (printed p.422, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.437","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-032-2","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MosT effective way of treatment in older children with nocturnal enuresis is","o":["motivational therapy","conditioning therapy","desmopressin acetate","oxybutynin chloride","imipramine"],"k":1,"ex":"This form of therapy has a reported success of 30-60%, although the relapse rate is significant, while motivational therapy; there is no evidence that this approach is beneficial, Pharmacologic therapy is intended to treat the symptom of enuresis and thus is regarded as second line and is not curative.","more":"Options:\n- A. motivational therapy\n\n - B. conditioning therapy\n - C. desmopressin acetate\n - D. oxybutynin chloride\n - E. imipramine","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF pp.432–433 (printed p.422, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.437","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-038","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MosT common cause of testicular pain in a 12- yr-old boy is","o":["epididymitis","testicular tumor *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","testicular torsion","scrotal hematoma *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","incarcerated inguinal hernia"],"k":2,"ex":"The source key identifies option C (testicular torsion) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"Options:\n- A. epididymitis\n\n - B. testicular tumor *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - C. testicular torsion\n - D. scrotal hematoma *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - E. incarcerated inguinal hernia","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF pp.433–434 (printed p.423, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.438","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":38,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-042","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MOsT accurate study in a child with suspected renal stone is","o":["renal ultra-sonogram","plain radiograph of the abdomen *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*","an unenhanced spiral CT scan","radioisotope studies","magnetic resonance urography"],"k":2,"ex":"This study takes only a few minutes to perform, has 96% sensitivity and specificity in delineating the number and location of calculi, and demonstrates whether the involved kidney is hydronephrotic or not.","more":"Options:\n- A. renal ultra-sonogram\n\n - B. plain radiograph of the abdomen *[option letter lost in OCR; assigned by sequence]*\n - C. an unenhanced spiral CT scan\n - D. radioisotope studies\n - E. magnetic resonance urography","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.434 (printed p.424, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.438","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":42,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-NEPHROLOGY-046","spec":"urology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Struvite calculi (secondary to urinary tract infections) are MOsT likely caused by","o":["Escherichia coli","Proteus","Klebsiella","Pseudomonas","Staph. aurous"],"k":1,"ex":"Caused by urea-splitting organisms (most often Proteus spp., and occasionally Klebsiella spp., Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas spp., and others).","more":"Options:\n- A. Escherichia coli\n\n - B. Proteus\n - C. Klebsiella\n - D. Pseudomonas\n - E. Staph. aurous","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs, Urologic Disorders in Infants and Children chapter, PDF p.435 (printed p.425, verified from page footer); answer key PDF p.438","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Nephrology","nlNumber":46,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-002","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (associations)","q":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a recognized association with","o":["infant of diabetic mother","Marfan syndrome","William syndrome","trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)","XO (Turner syndrome)"],"k":0,"ex":"Infants of diabetic mothers frequently show transient asymmetric septal hypertrophy (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy picture) due to fetal hyperinsulinism; it usually regresses over weeks to months. Other recognised HCM associations include Noonan syndrome, Friedreich ataxia and metabolic disease.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer). Infant of diabetic mother – HCM/septal hypertrophy.\n\n- **B.** Marfan syndrome – aortic root dilatation/dissection, aortic regurgitation, mitral valve prolapse.\n- **C.** Williams syndrome – supravalvular aortic stenosis and peripheral pulmonary stenosis.\n- **D.** Down syndrome – AV septal defect, VSD, ASD, PDA.\n- **E.** Turner syndrome (XO) – coarctation of the aorta, bicuspid aortic valve, aortic stenosis.\n\nSource explanation:\nMarfan syndrome is associated with dilated and dissecting aorta, aortic valve regurgitation, mitral valve prolapsed, William syndrome with supravalvular aortic stenosis, peripheral pulmonary stenosis, Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) with endocardial cushion defect, VSD, ASD, PDA, while XO (Turner syndrome) associated with coarctation of aorta, aortic stenosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-003","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis (innocent murmur)","q":"The following are characters of venous hum ExCEPT","o":["continuous murmur","heard in infraclavicular region","grades I-IlI/VI","louder with patient in supine position","changes with compression of jugular vein"],"k":3,"ex":"Venous hum is a benign continuous murmur of turbulent jugular venous flow, heard in the infraclavicular/supraclavicular region (usually right), grade I–III/VI, typically age 3–8 years. It is LOUDER sitting/upright and disappears when the child lies supine, with head turning, or with gentle jugular compression.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** True – continuous murmur (systolic and diastolic components).\n\n- **B.** True – infraclavicular (and supraclavicular) region.\n- **C.** True – soft, grade I–III/VI.\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT). It is louder upright, not supine – the source answer is D.\n- **E.** True – compression of the jugular vein abolishes it.\n\nSource explanation:\nLouder with patient in upright position.\n\nHigh-yield:\nInnocent-murmur rule: variation with position + normal exam/ECG/echo = reassurance.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-005","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Congenital heart disease causing cyanosis without respiratory distress include the following EXCEPT","o":["tricuspid atresia","aortic stenosis","Ebstein anomaly","pulmonary atresia","tetralogy of Fallot"],"k":1,"ex":"Cyanotic lesions with decreased pulmonary blood flow (TOF, tricuspid atresia, pulmonary atresia, Ebstein anomaly) produce cyanosis with little or no respiratory distress ('blue and quiet' babies). Aortic stenosis is an acyanotic obstructive lesion; critical AS presents with heart failure/shock, not cyanosis.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Tricuspid atresia – cyanosis, decreased pulmonary flow, no distress.\n\n- **B.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – aortic stenosis is acyanotic (letter lost in OCR; position B; source answer B).\n- **C.** Ebstein anomaly – cyanosis from right-to-left atrial shunt.\n- **D.** Pulmonary atresia – duct-dependent cyanosis (letter lost in OCR; position D).\n- **E.** Tetralogy of Fallot – commonest cyanotic CHD beyond infancy; cyanosis without distress.\n\nSource explanation:\nNone provided by the source (the key line gives the letter only).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: option letters B and D are missing; options identified by position (A–E in order). Source answer 'B' fits the text.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":5,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-007","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis (ECG)","q":"The ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy and a superior QRS axls (between 0° and -90) in","o":["tricuspid atresia","Ebstein anomaly",".pulmonary atresia","pulmonary stenosis","tetralogy of Fallot"],"k":0,"ex":"Tricuspid atresia classically shows left axis deviation/superior axis (0° to −90°) with left ventricular hypertrophy because only the left ventricle is functional and the right ventricle is hypoplastic. Most other cyanotic lesions show right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer A) – tricuspid atresia: superior axis + LVH.\n\n- **B.** Ebstein anomaly – right atrial enlargement, RBBB/right axis, WPW possible; not superior axis with LVH.\n- **C.** Pulmonary atresia (letter lost; position C) – with intact septum may show LVH but axis is normal/right in most cases; not the classic superior-axis lesion.\n- **D.** Pulmonary stenosis – right axis deviation, RVH.\n- **E.** Tetralogy of Fallot – RVH, right axis deviation.\n\nSource explanation:\nNone provided by the source (the key line gives the letter only).\n\nHigh-yield:\nCyanosis + left axis deviation = tricuspid atresia (AV septal defect also gives superior axis).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source key '7.(A)' has no explanatory text. OCR: option letters lost and 'axls' typo; text otherwise readable.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":7,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-008","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (epidemiology)","q":"The MosT common cause of death from cardiac defects in the first month of life is","o":["d-Transposition without associated lesions","hypoplastic left heart syndrome","pulmonary atresia","truncus arteriosus","complex single ventricle"],"k":1,"ex":"Hypoplastic left heart syndrome (HLHS) is about 1% of CHD but is the commonest cardiac cause of death in the first month of life: duct-dependent systemic circulation, collapse and acidosis when the ductus closes. Prostaglandin E1 keeps the duct open pending staged (Norwood) surgery or transplant.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** d-TGA without associated lesions – serious but treatable (PGE1, septostomy, arterial switch).\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – HLHS.\n- **C.** Pulmonary atresia – duct-dependent, treated with PGE1/shunt.\n- **D.** Truncus arteriosus – usually survives the first weeks; presents with heart failure.\n- **E.** Complex single ventricle – variable course.\n\nSource explanation:\nHypoplastic left heart syndrome accounts for 1% of all congenital heart defects but is the most common cause of death from cardiac defects in the first month of life.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-009","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"All the following are causes of heart failure in full-term neonate ExcEPT","o":["asphyxial cardiomyopathy","coarctation of aorta","hypoplastic left heart","transposition of great arteries","ventricular septal defect"],"k":4,"ex":"In the full-term neonate, heart failure in the first days comes from severe left ventricular outflow obstruction (HLHS, critical AS, coarctation), TGA and myocardial causes (asphyxial cardiomyopathy, arrhythmia, myocarditis). An isolated VSD does not cause failure in the first days because pulmonary resistance is still high; it presents at about 4–8 weeks as resistance falls.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Asphyxial cardiomyopathy – recognised neonatal cause.\n\n- **B.** Coarctation of the aorta – duct closure causes failure/shock.\n- **C.** HLHS – duct-dependent circulation; early failure/collapse.\n- **D.** TGA – failure/cyanosis early.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – VSD (source answer E): failure appears later in infancy.\n\nSource explanation:\nFluid overload, PDA, VSD, and Cor pulmonale (BPD) are causes of heart failure in premature neonate.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":9,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-010","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"One of the following is a sign of right-sided heart failure","o":["edema","tachypnea","orthopnea","wheezing","pulmonary edema"],"k":0,"ex":"Right-sided failure produces systemic venous congestion: hepatomegaly, peripheral/dependent oedema and distended neck veins. Left-sided failure produces pulmonary venous congestion: tachypnoea, orthopnoea, wheeze and pulmonary oedema.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – oedema (with hepatomegaly, neck-vein distension) reflects right-sided failure.\n\n- **B.** Tachypnoea – left-sided/pulmonary congestion.\n- **C.** Orthopnoea – left-sided failure.\n- **D.** Wheezing ('cardiac asthma') – left-sided failure.\n- **E.** Pulmonary oedema – left-sided failure.\n\nSource explanation:\nIf left-sided failure is predominant, tachypnea, orthopnea, wheezing, and pulmonary edema are seen. Hepatomegaly, edema, and distended neck veins are signs of right-sided failure.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-011","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The MosT common cause of syncope in children is","o":["Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","prolonged QT syndrome","atrioventricular block","neurocardiogenic syncope","myocarditis"],"k":3,"ex":"Neurocardiogenic (vasovagal/neurally mediated) syncope is by far the commonest cause in otherwise healthy children and adolescents; it follows triggers such as prolonged standing, fear or blood draws, with a prodrome (nausea, sweating, dizziness). Cardiac causes (arrhythmia, outflow obstruction, long-QT syndrome) are rare but must be excluded by history (exertional syncope, palpitations, family history of sudden death), examination and ECG.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Wolff-Parkinson-White – rare cause (SVT-related).\n\n- **B.** Prolonged QT syndrome – rare but dangerous; exertional syncope/family history.\n- **C.** AV block – rare.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – neurocardiogenic syncope.\n- **E.** Myocarditis – rare cause.\n\nSource explanation:\nIn otherwise healthy children, neurocardiogenic syncope is most common. This entity goes by a number of terms, including vasovagal syncope, neurally mediated syncope, and autonomic syncope.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":11,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-012","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Of the following, the MosT common clinical sign of coarctation of the aorta in older children is","o":["cardiac enlargement","notching of the inferior border of the ribs","a systolic ejection click or thrill in the suprasternal notch","differential blood pressure: arms > legs","diminished or absent femoral or lower-extremity pulses"],"k":3,"ex":"In older children the commonest sign of coarctation is a blood-pressure difference – higher in the arms than in the legs (in healthy people leg pressure is slightly higher). Weak or delayed femoral pulses with radio-femoral delay are the other classic findings, so BP should be measured in all four limbs.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cardiac enlargement – not typical in older children; seen with severe infantile coarctation and failure.\n\n- **B.** Rib notching – a radiological (not clinical) sign, usually after 6 years.\n- **C.** Ejection click/thrill in suprasternal notch – suggests a bicuspid aortic valve/aortic stenosis.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – arm BP higher than leg BP.\n- **E.** Diminished/absent femoral pulses – a classic sign too, but the source keys differential BP as the most common.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe most common clinical sign of coarctation of the aorta in older children is differential blood pressure: arms > legs (100%).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Note for reviewer: option E is also a classic sign; the stem says 'most common' and the source explanation keys D (100%). OCR: question number appears as '1.' for 12 (confirmed by sequence and by the answer key '12.(D)').","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":12,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-013","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The chest x-ray may help to differentiate the types of congenital heart defects. Increased pulmonary markings (increased pulmonary blood flow) is seen in","o":["pulmonary atresia","truncus arteriosus","tetralogy of Fallot","tricuspid atresia","Ebstein anomaly"],"k":1,"ex":"Lesions with increased pulmonary blood flow (plethoric lungs) include left-to-right shunts, TGA, TAPVR and truncus arteriosus. Lesions with decreased flow (oligaemic lungs) include pulmonary atresia/severe PS, TOF, tricuspid atresia and Ebstein anomaly.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Pulmonary atresia – oligaemic.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – truncus arteriosus: increased flow.\n- **C.** Tetralogy of Fallot – oligaemic, boot-shaped heart.\n- **D.** Tricuspid atresia – oligaemic (usually).\n- **E.** Ebstein anomaly – oligaemic, box-shaped heart.\n\nSource explanation:\nDecreased pulmonary markings (diminished pulmonary blood flow) > Pulmonary atresia or severe stenosis Tetralogy of Fallot Tricuspid atresia > Ebstein anomaly Increased pulmonary markings (increased pulmonary blood flow) Transposition of great arteries Total anomalous pulmonary venous return Truncus arteriosus","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source answer '13.(B)' is followed by an OCR'd table (decreased vs increased markings) that confirms the grouping. No answer problem.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":13,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-015","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"SVT in children differ from physiologic sinus tachycardia by all the following ExCEPT","o":["sudden onset and termination","8. persistent ventricular rate of >180 bpm","fixed RR interval on ECG","abnormal P-wave shape or axis or absent P waves","evident change in heart rate with activity"],"k":4,"ex":"SVT starts and stops abruptly, has a fixed, very rapid rate (≈ 220–300/min in infants; > 180/min in children) with no beat-to-beat variability, and P waves are abnormal or absent. Sinus tachycardia varies with crying, fever and activity and has normal P waves.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** SVT feature – sudden onset and termination.\n\n- **B.** SVT feature – persistent rapid ventricular rate (letter shown as '8.' in OCR; position B).\n- **C.** SVT feature – fixed R-R interval.\n- **D.** SVT feature – abnormal/absent P waves.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – rate change with activity is a sinus tachycardia feature (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nLittle change in heart rate with activity, crying, or breath holding.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: option B letter appears as '8.'. Rate cut-offs (>180) are as in the source; infants usually exceed 220/min.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":15,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-016","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Prevention","q":"The following cardiac lesions are at increased risk for bacterial endocarditis ExCEPT","o":["mitral insufficiency","aortic stenosis","atrial septal defect seccundum","coarctation of the aorta","patent ductus arteriosus"],"k":2,"ex":"Nearly all structural heart lesions predispose to infective endocarditis (IE) except secundum ASD, whose low-velocity left-to-right flow does not injure endocardium. Regurgitant valves, AS (often bicuspid), coarctation and PDA are higher-risk.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Mitral insufficiency – risk present.\n\n- **B.** Aortic stenosis – risk present.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – secundum ASD, not at increased risk (source answer C).\n- **D.** Coarctation of the aorta – risk present.\n- **E.** PDA – endothelial injury by jet; risk present even when small.\n\nSource explanation:\nNone provided by the source (the key line gives the letter only).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source answer '16.(C)' has no explanatory text; consistent with the curriculum. OCR: 'seccundum' typo.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":16,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-018","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"The following are major side effects of PGE, EXCEPT","o":["apnea","fever","cutaneous flushing","seizures","hypertension"],"k":4,"ex":"PGE1 maintains ductal patency in duct-dependent CHD. Main adverse effects: apnoea (most important, especially in small neonates – be ready to ventilate), fever, cutaneous flushing, seizures/jitteriness, hypotension from vasodilation, bradycardia/tachycardia.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Apnoea – major side effect.\n\n- **B.** Fever – recognised side effect.\n- **C.** Flushing – recognised (vasodilation).\n- **D.** Seizures/jitteriness – recognised.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – hypertension (source answer E); the drug causes hypotension.\n\nSource explanation:\nApnea, fever, cutaneous flushing, seizures, hypotension, and bradycardia/tachycardia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: 'PGE,' (probably PGE1; subscript lost). Source explanation text is fragmented but consistent.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":18,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-020","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"In MOsT patients with an ASD, the characteristic physical finding is","o":["a right ventricular systolic lift","a fixed splitted 2nd heart sound","a systolic ejection murmur","a short, rumbling mid-diastolic murmur","a mild left precordial bulge"],"k":1,"ex":"The hallmark of ASD is a widely split second heart sound that is FIXED (does not vary with respiration), because right ventricular stroke volume is equal in inspiration and expiration. A soft ejection systolic murmur at the upper left sternal border (increased flow across the pulmonary valve) and a mid-diastolic tricuspid flow murmur may be present, but the fixed split S2 is characteristic.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Right ventricular systolic lift – occurs in large shunts but is not the characteristic sign.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – wide, fixed split S2.\n- **C.** Systolic ejection murmur – common but non-specific (pulmonary flow murmur).\n- **D.** Short mid-diastolic rumble – with large shunts (relative tricuspid stenosis); not the characteristic sign.\n- **E.** Left precordial bulge – with large long-standing shunts.\n\nSource explanation:\nIn most patients with an ASD, the characteristic finding is that the 2nd heart sound is widely split and fixed in its splitting during all phases of respiration.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":20,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-023","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"All the following are signs of coarctation of the aorta ExCEPT","o":["femoral pulses are weak or absent","t bounding pulses of the arms","femoral pulse occurs slightly before the radial pulse","blood pressure in the legs is lower than that in the arms","precordial impulse and heart sounds are usually normal"],"k":2,"ex":"In coarctation the femoral pulses are weak, delayed or absent, with radio-femoral delay (blood reaches the legs through collaterals). Arm pulses are bounding and arm BP is higher than leg BP; the precordial impulse and heart sounds are usually normal.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Weak or absent femoral pulses – sign.\n\n- **B.** Bounding arm pulses – sign (OCR shows a stray 't' before this option).\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – femoral pulse slightly before radial is NORMAL; in coarctation the femoral is delayed (source answer C).\n- **D.** Leg BP lower than arm BP – sign.\n- **E.** Normal precordial impulse and heart sounds – usual in older children.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe radial and femoral pulses should always be palpated simultaneously for the presence of a radial-femoral delay. Normally, the femoral pulse occurs slightly before the radial pulse. A radial-femoral delay occurs when blood flow to the descending aorta is dependent on collaterals, in which case the femoral pulse is felt after the radial pulse.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Minor OCR: stray 't' in option B.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":23,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-027","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The following procedures may abort an attack of SVT ExCEpT A","o":["placing of the face in ice water","straining","breath holding","standing on head","ocular pressure 1"],"k":4,"ex":"Vagal manoeuvres terminate many SVT episodes (≈ 80% success): ice-water/ice-bag to the face in infants (diving reflex), Valsalva/straining, breath holding and similar manoeuvres. Ocular (eyeball) pressure must never be used – it risks retinal injury and is abandoned; carotid sinus massage is rarely effective and is not advised in infants.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Placing the face in ice water – effective vagal stimulation (diving reflex).\n\n- **B.** Straining – Valsalva-type vagal stimulation.\n- **C.** Breath holding – vagal stimulation (letter lost; position C).\n- **D.** Standing on head – listed by the source as a manoeuvre that may abort SVT.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – ocular pressure should never be performed (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nOcular pressure must never be performed, and carotid sinus massage is very rarely effective.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: stray 'A', '1', '4' characters near option A and the page break; option letter C lost. Option D wording ('standing on head') is as OCR'd; the source key does not depend on it.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":27,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-029","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (epidemiology)","q":"The MosT common cause of death in competitive athletes is","o":["aortic stenosis","hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Ccoronary artery abnormalities","myocarditis","long Q-T syndrome (LQTS)"],"k":1,"ex":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, with or without left ventricular outflow obstruction, is the commonest cause of sudden cardiac death in young competitive athletes (ventricular arrhythmia on exertion). Others: coronary artery anomalies, myocarditis, long QT, aortic stenosis, Marfan aortic rupture.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Aortic stenosis – less common cause.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – HCM.\n- **C.** Coronary artery abnormalities – second most common in many series.\n- **D.** Myocarditis – less common.\n- **E.** Long QT syndrome – less common.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe most common cause of death in competitive athletes is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, with or without obstruction to left ventricular outflow.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":29,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-030","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (microbiology)","q":"The leading causative agents for endocarditis in pediatric patients are","o":["group D enterococci","viridans-type streptococci","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","fungal organisms","Serratia marcescens"],"k":1,"ex":"Viridans-group (α-haemolytic) streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus are the leading causes of infective endocarditis in children; viridans streptococci predominate in subacute disease on abnormal valves, S. aureus in acute disease and in children with normal valves/central lines. Enterococci, HACEK, Pseudomonas and fungi are less common.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Group D enterococci – GI/GU sources; less common.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – viridans-type streptococci.\n- **C.** Pseudomonas aeruginosa – IV drug use; uncommon.\n- **D.** Fungi – immunocompromised hosts.\n- **E.** Serratia marcescens – rare.\n\nSource explanation:\nViridans-type streptococci (a-hemolytic streptococci) and Staphylococcus aureus remain the leading causative agents for endocarditis in pediatric patients.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":30,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-031","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The following is a major Duke criterion for the diagnosis of endocarditis","o":["new valve regurgitant flow by echocardiography","Osler nodes","single positive blood culture","serologic evidence of infection","high erythrocyte sedimentation rate"],"k":0,"ex":"Major Duke criteria: (1) positive blood cultures with typical organisms (two separate cultures) and (2) echocardiographic evidence of endocardial involvement (vegetation/intracardiac mass, abscess, new partial dehiscence of a prosthesis, or new valvular regurgitation). Minor criteria include predisposition, fever, vascular/embolic phenomena, immunological phenomena (Osler nodes, glomerulonephritis, serology) and a single positive culture.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – new valve regurgitant flow on echo (major).\n\n- **B.** Osler nodes – minor (immunological) criterion.\n- **C.** Single positive blood culture – minor (needs ≥2 for major).\n- **D.** Serological evidence of infection – minor.\n- **E.** High ESR – not a criterion in the modified Duke scheme.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe Duke criteria help in the diagnosis of endocarditis. Major criteria include (1) positive blood cultures (2 separate cultures for a usual pathogen, 2 or more for less typical pathogens), and (2) evidence of endocarditis on echocardiography (intracardiac mass on a valve or other site, regurgitant flow near a prosthesis, abscess, partial dehiscence of prosthetic valves, or new valve regurgitant flow).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":31,"f":false}]);